WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.362
Open WebUI becomes useful when it is connected to model backends.

00:00:04.362 --> 00:00:11.610
Those backends may be local inference services, remote APIs, or OpenAI-compatible endpoints at a high level.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:25.435
The connection usually depends on an endpoint address, supported API shape, authentication details, available model names, network reachability, and settings such as context length or generation parameters.

00:00:25.435 --> 00:00:28.321
Model selection should be tied to use case.

00:00:28.321 --> 00:00:38.186
A lightweight local model may be fine for casual drafting, while document analysis, code help, or structured extraction may need a different model.

00:00:38.186 --> 00:00:44.024
Temperature and sampling settings influence how deterministic or varied responses feel.

00:00:44.024 --> 00:00:53.755
Context length affects how much conversation and supporting material can be considered, but larger context can increase resource use and latency.

00:00:53.755 --> 00:00:57.245
Connectivity testing should be basic and repeatable.

00:00:57.245 --> 00:01:08.654
Confirm that the endpoint is reachable from the Open WebUI host, that credentials are valid, that the expected models appear, and that a simple prompt returns a response.

00:01:08.654 --> 00:01:19.928
Failed connections may come from network binding, firewall rules, incorrect base URLs, unavailable runtimes, invalid keys, missing models, or incompatible API behavior.

00:01:19.928 --> 00:01:22.546
Document approved models and endpoints.

00:01:22.546 --> 00:01:33.552
Operators should know which endpoints are local, which are remote, which use private data, which are experimental, and which are appropriate for specific workflows.

00:01:33.552 --> 00:01:39.256
Documentation reduces confusion when models are renamed, moved, upgraded, or retired.

00:01:39.256 --> 00:01:43.954
It also helps users avoid sending sensitive work to the wrong backend.
